{"title":"Menelaus' Theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Menelaus' Theorem</b> deals with the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Collinearity\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Collinearity\">collinearity</a> of points on each of the three sides (extended when necessary) of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangle\" title=\"Triangle\">triangle</a>. It is named after Menelaus of Alexandria.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Proposition\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proposition</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proof_with_Areas\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof with Areas</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof_with_Similar_Triangles\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof with Similar Triangles</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Proof_with_Barycentric_coordinates\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof with Barycentric coordinates</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Proof_with_Mass_points\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof with Mass points</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Converse\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Converse</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proposition\">Proposition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Proposition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose a transversal line cuts the sides (interior or exterior) of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> at the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/f/3efd92d7eef6e4b04b61b6ff7e353462a2607585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> such that neither <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/f/3efd92d7eef6e4b04b61b6ff7e353462a2607585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> are equal to points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. Then, the figure must satisfy\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/3/6d345cd2827d3765dd933aab8e77729629fe2c02.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP}{PC} \\cdot \\frac{CQ}{QA} \\cdot \\frac{AR}{RB} = 1\\]\" width=\"154\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/f/76f577bb2e7530cfad3e26001b2052ae178aa0d2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(16); defaultpen(fontsize(8)); pair A=(7,6), B=(0,0), C=(10,0), P=(4,0), Q=(6,8), R; draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(7,6)--(0,0),blue+0.75); draw((7,6)--(6,8)--(4,0)); R=intersectionpoint(A--B,Q--P); dot(A^^B^^C^^P^^Q^^R); label(&quot;A&quot;,A,(1,1));label(&quot;B&quot;,B,(-1,0));label(&quot;C&quot;,C,(1,0));label(&quot;P&quot;,P,(0,-1));label(&quot;Q&quot;,Q,N);label(&quot;R&quot;,R,(-1,1)); [/asy]\" width=\"302\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>Prominently, when written with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Directed_segment\" title=\"Directed segment\">directed segment</a> or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector\" title=\"Vector\">vectors</a>, the theorem states\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/2/4026782819cab389a35ee162841d829d63fe9e3a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP}{PC} \\cdot \\frac{CQ}{QA} \\cdot \\frac{AR}{RB} = -1\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"41\" />\nThe theorem also works when all three points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/f/3efd92d7eef6e4b04b61b6ff7e353462a2607585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> lie on the extension of the triangle's sides. Such a configuration is shown below.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/8/828febef7635b2c17ac95ac4116193d659ff3b37.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(16); defaultpen(fontsize(8)); pair A=(7,6), B=(0,0), C=(10,0), P=(18,0), Q=(6,8), R; draw(B--C--A--B, blue); draw(C--P, blue + dashed); draw(A--Q, blue + dashed); R = extension(A,B,P,Q); draw(A--R, blue + dashed); draw(Q--P); dot(A^^B^^C^^P^^Q^^R); label(&quot;A&quot;,A,(-1,0));label(&quot;B&quot;,B,(-1,-1));label(&quot;C&quot;,C,(0,-1));label(&quot;P&quot;,P,(0,-1));label(&quot;Q&quot;,Q,(0,1));label(&quot;R&quot;,R,(1,1)); [/asy]\" width=\"505\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>The theorem is complemented by its converse too.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_with_Areas\">Proof with Areas</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof with Areas\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Through area ratio evaluations followed by the subtractive property of equivalent ratios,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c75326cce242f3086b292d40bae4587124b748e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP}{PC} = \\frac{[\\triangle BPQ]}{[\\triangle PCQ]} = \\frac{[\\triangle BPR]}{[\\triangle PCR]} \\implies \\frac{BP}{PC} = \\frac{[\\triangle BQR]}{[\\triangle CQR]}\\]\" width=\"404\" height=\"43\" />\nLikewise, the other ratios turn out to be\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/b/4fb83e84ef9db6e0058bf815ce78bd5d7ad8a2dc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{CQ}{QA} = \\frac{[\\triangle CQR]}{[\\triangle AQR]}\\]\" width=\"126\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/2/63237dfc338aff725f0c6908dc40fb9f0263e2ef.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{AR}{RB} = \\frac{[\\triangle AQR]}{[\\triangle BQR]}\\]\" width=\"126\" height=\"43\" />\nTherefore, it is evident that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/3/6d345cd2827d3765dd933aab8e77729629fe2c02.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP}{PC} \\cdot \\frac{CQ}{QA} \\cdot \\frac{AR}{RB} = 1\\]\" width=\"154\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_with_Similar_Triangles\">Proof with Similar Triangles</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof with Similar Triangles\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Draw a line parallel to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/e/3be5b2b1c5702a00b8442d62d03534e3a0a0f4be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" /> through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to intersect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/a/21a5638c7f244737d8cab27dad348acb8d143023.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(16); defaultpen(fontsize(8)); pair A=(7,6), B=(0,0), C=(10,0), P=(4,0), Q=(6,8), R, K=(5.5,0); draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(7,6)--(0,0),blue+0.75); draw((7,6)--(6,8)--(4,0)); draw(A--K, dashed); R=intersectionpoint(A--B,Q--P); dot(A^^B^^C^^P^^Q^^R^^K); label(&quot;A&quot;,A,(1,1));label(&quot;B&quot;,B,(-1,0));label(&quot;C&quot;,C,(1,0));label(&quot;P&quot;,P,(0,-1));label(&quot;Q&quot;,Q,(1,0));label(&quot;R&quot;,R,(-1,1)); label(&quot;K&quot;,K,(0,-1)); [/asy]\" width=\"302\" height=\"238\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/4/ee41df30a4acdc4b10785867a0095d280f88c109.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\triangle RBP \\sim \\triangle ABK \\implies \\frac{AR}{RB}=\\frac{KP}{PB}\\]\" width=\"283\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbce799ea70e0ca9cdaeedf58e29beb1153ec1a2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\triangle QCP \\sim \\triangle ACK \\implies \\frac{QC}{QA}=\\frac{CP}{KP}\\]\" width=\"282\" height=\"40\" />\nMultiplying the two equalities together to eliminate the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/e/8cef65b28328af6d55985daf18f0d621ef761bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PK$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> factor, we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/0/370f69e4b80e4b49c3af8e34a21e034399ce8963.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{AR}{RB}\\cdot\\frac{QC}{QA}=\\frac{CP}{PB}\\implies \\frac{AR}{RB}\\cdot\\frac{QC}{QA}\\cdot\\frac{PB}{CP}=1\\]\" width=\"340\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_with_Barycentric_coordinates\">Proof with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Barycentric_coordinates\" title=\"Barycentric coordinates\">Barycentric coordinates</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Proof with Barycentric coordinates\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Disclaimer: This proof is not nearly as elegant as the above one. It uses a bash-type approach, as barycentric coordinate proofs tend to be. \n</p><p>Suppose we give the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/1/b31cab458dc17cdaa49b467c836004d1738cae97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P, Q, R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> the following coordinates:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/3/113f0d7ae3674d3c6d95b2075c8ea6a378f1315e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P: (0, P, 1-P)\\]\" width=\"124\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/2523e899e49212242c7416e4a40b328805912da4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R: (R , 1-R, 0)\\]\" width=\"125\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/3/41380ea748030a8d2c05f23321719e65d096b5d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Q: (1-Q ,0 , Q)\\]\" width=\"127\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Note that this says the following:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/e/d7e7e694774acf38f43d0cf4f8440a1256610109.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{CP}{PB}=\\frac{1-P}{P}\\]\" width=\"103\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/4/28460d86a1d7af2927a0590782d0cd1ad8410db0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BR}{AR}=\\frac{1-R}{R}\\]\" width=\"102\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad29e7b5a567080756f3b7a459740398c76b879f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{QA}{QC}=\\frac{1-Q}{Q}\\]\" width=\"103\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>The line through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is given by:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/d/14d4e372cdb043b6d23a6685f6f2c569dc27cc16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{vmatrix} X &amp; 0 &amp; R \\\\ Y &amp; P &amp; 1-R\\\\ Z &amp; 1-P &amp; 0 \\end{vmatrix} = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -28px\" width=\"185\" height=\"65\" />\n</p><p>which yields, after simplification, \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f138fb129502bfa2bd23ea25d58a62447fa1ed56.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[-X\\cdot (R-1)(P-1)+Y\\cdot R(1-P)-Z\\cdot PR = 0\\]\" width=\"395\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced00b57885e612b42ef65989dc51362524388e2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Z\\cdot PR = -X\\cdot (R-1)(P-1)+Y\\cdot R(1-P).\\]\" width=\"368\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Plugging in the coordinates for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/2/7d2be2e4af9308d0a542cfb12d05dd48ac804f3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(Q-1)(R-1)(P-1) = QPR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"245\" height=\"18\" />. From <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/0/4c0408df478f21805a91a99f49a34d11013294d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{CP}{PB}=\\frac{1-P}{P},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"110\" height=\"37\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/4/014878e5d4964845c637419c2985c4674b4a7929.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P=\\frac{(1-P)\\cdot PB}{CP}.\\]\" width=\"147\" height=\"38\" /> Likewise, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/7/3a7abff5351fd621f2d4d4fc42ed9ee84e1ae21f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R=\\frac{(1-R)\\cdot AR}{BR}\\]\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/4/f448a124aae74987854ffa8a13bd7738f0ef843c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Q=\\frac{(1-Q)\\cdot QC}{QA}.\\]\" width=\"147\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p><br />\nSubstituting these values yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/9/7a9fab55394000872444c52778e20f24fe9f248c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(Q-1)(R-1)(P-1) = \\frac{(1-Q)\\cdot QC \\cdot (1-P) \\cdot PB \\cdot (1-R) \\cdot AR}{QA\\cdot CP\\cdot BR}\\]\" width=\"509\" height=\"42\" /> which simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/2/3c298bf71286d6e2794a3cab0cd7bd5c35d7e06a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QA\\cdot CP \\cdot BR = -QC \\cdot AR \\cdot PB.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"264\" height=\"16\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_with_Mass_points\">Proof with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mass_points\" title=\"Mass points\">Mass points</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Proof with Mass points\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>First let's define some masses.  \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/f/52f120a7a2df6d15adde53e1749254bcca8763c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_{m_{1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"31\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/8/8d80d6be562aa805b20fb2720a05bd2d49da2703.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_{m_{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/e/61e2b4ee56b15fe18862201985171a499fe47e5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_{m_{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>By Mass Points: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/7/827ce735c104e13238e4096a890c6d2ee9ba005e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[BP\\cdot m_{1}=PC\\cdot m_{2} \\implies \\frac{BP}{CP}=\\frac{m_{2}}{m_{1}}\\]\" width=\"286\" height=\"40\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/5/c354156ef9729688fcf3b1e69ff2ab952c943ef5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{QC}{QA}=\\frac{AC+QA}{QA}=1+\\frac{AC}{QA}=1+\\frac{m_{3}}{m_{2}}=\\frac{m_{2}}{m_{2}}+\\frac{m_{3}}{m_{2}}=\\frac{m_{3}+m_{2}}{m_{2}}\\]\" width=\"480\" height=\"41\" />\nThe mass at A is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/8/9280466b8c150b949025e82b7920958e2862cce7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m_{3}+m_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"67\" height=\"13\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/8/e583221b9103be1781d5901a0e89323b5c7420f4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AR\\cdot (m_{3}+m_{2}) = RB \\cdot m_{1} \\implies \\frac{AR}{RB} = \\frac{m_{1}}{m_{3}+m_{2}}\\]\" width=\"391\" height=\"40\" />\nMultiplying them together,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/b/18bde7f73ba8b6ae5a2bf0265d1055e19bf5adfe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\;\\; \\frac{BP}{CP} \\cdot \\frac{QC}{QA} \\cdot \\frac{AR}{RB} = \\frac{{m_{2}}}{{m_{1}}} \\cdot \\frac{{m_{3}+m_{2}}}{{m_{2}}} \\cdot \\frac{{m_{1}}}{{m_{3}+m_{2}}} = 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"390\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Converse\">Converse</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Converse\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The converse of Menelaus' theorem is also true.  If the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/f/3efd92d7eef6e4b04b61b6ff7e353462a2607585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> on the sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> satisfy the directed length or vector equality\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/2/4026782819cab389a35ee162841d829d63fe9e3a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP}{PC} \\cdot \\frac{CQ}{QA} \\cdot \\frac{AR}{RB} = -1\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"41\" />\nthen <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/1/b31cab458dc17cdaa49b467c836004d1738cae97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P, Q, R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> must be <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Collinear\" title=\"Collinear\">collinear</a>.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/e/87e7bd56f19d2bc7aa2f8aa9681e95b0d546ef76.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(16); defaultpen(fontsize(8)); pair A=(7,6), B=(0,0), C=(10,0), P=(4,0), Q=(6,8), R; draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(7,6)--(0,0),blue+0.75); draw((7,6)--(6,8)--(4,0)); R=intersectionpoint(A--B,Q--P); dot(A^^B^^C^^P^^Q^^R); label(&quot;A&quot;,A,(1,1));label(&quot;B&quot;,B,(-1,0));label(&quot;C&quot;,C,(1,0));label(&quot;P&quot;,P,(0,-1));label(&quot;Q&quot;,Q,(1,0));label(&quot;R&quot;,R,(-1,1)); [/asy]\" width=\"302\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>The converse is particularly useful in proving the collinearity of three points on multiple occasions. Note that the converse is strictly based on the directed length or vector equality; using magnitudes instead will not cause the converse to hold at all times because of the existence of harmonic conjugates.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><i>For the sake of contradiction</i>, suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/f/3efd92d7eef6e4b04b61b6ff7e353462a2607585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> aren't collinear. Assume line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/7/9a7c52aff9dd9987076c0ac4fecccf59b6ed8bdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" /> intersects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/4/3545551b3d62c4e23d5df7352a55602d0aa9adcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P&#39;$\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> instead. By the proposition of Menelaus' theorem,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/4/5c4e2c21aa0babb8102a7549efb132466b52f73f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP&#39;}{P&#39;C} \\cdot \\frac{CQ}{QA} \\cdot \\frac{AR}{RB} = -1\\]\" width=\"173\" height=\"41\" />\nand since it is known that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/2/4026782819cab389a35ee162841d829d63fe9e3a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP}{PC} \\cdot \\frac{CQ}{QA} \\cdot \\frac{AR}{RB} = -1\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"41\" />\nequating them yields\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/7/227a199e80599beee8a2cc967efa3312c26e1777.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP&#39;}{P&#39;C} = \\frac{BP}{PC}\\]\" width=\"91\" height=\"38\" />\nHowever, applying the subtractive property of equivalent ratios suggests\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35d274184477ea378f2168e8dc21559b6e0c2d6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BP}{PC} = \\frac{PP&#39;}{P&#39;P} = -1\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"38\" />\nwhich is absurd because for any three collinear points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/f/79fff1317b48a57146bc1936964b9e381ee87824.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B,P,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/0/aa00238338adb5b6b053ee13ca99a8c4f58db357.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BP}{PC} \\ne -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"82\" height=\"39\" /> is always true. Therefore, our initial assumption that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/f/3efd92d7eef6e4b04b61b6ff7e353462a2607585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> isn't collinear must be false. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Menelaus%27_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ceva%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Ceva&#39;s Theorem\">Ceva's Theorem</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Stewart%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Stewart&#39;s Theorem\">Stewart's Theorem</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017181836\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.029 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.038 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 364/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 14616/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:1771:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017181836 and revision id 293382. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}